-//! P17 half A, decision 02: "sunk -> the terrain wins the depth test, the plate is simply hidden".
-//! That sentence is only true while the terrain the depth test uses is the LOD 0 heightfield. From LOD 1
-//! outwards the terrain mesh samples the heightmap every 2^level units, so between two sampled nodes it
-//! draws the CHORD over whatever is in between: a decimated surface that dips locally below the real
-//! ground by up to m_geomError, which is metre class on any interesting terrain (@see research 11 1.2).
-//! Wherever that dip goes under a sunken plate, the plate's own darker material wins the depth test and
-//! pokes through the ground in a scalloped band that grows with distance. It is not a shadow, not the
-//! bake (Raise mode never touches the terrain under a sunken plate) and not the base pass; the plate is
-//! simply visible where it was designed to be invisible.
-//! The engine already has the exact semantic for "do not draw this in camera passes, but keep everything
-//! else": ERF_HIDDEN_FROM_CAMERA (IRenderNode.h:225), tested in CBrush::Render (Brush.cpp:158, :820)
-//! only when the pass is neither a shadow pass nor a recursive one - so shadows, the static shadow
-//! cache, HeightMap AO, SVOGI voxelisation, physics, the navmesh and the editor's CPU hit test all keep
-//! working (@see research 10 3.2, which established this flag for step 2 of the base pass work).
-//! So: when the plate is entirely under the ground, stop its camera draw. At LOD 0 that changes nothing
-//! - the terrain was covering it anyway - and at every coarser LOD there is now nothing left to poke
-//! through. It is deliberately an ALL OR NOTHING test: a plate with any part above the terrain keeps
-//! drawing, because the crossing region is a per triangle problem and belongs to decision 06 C4.
-//! Escape hatch: e_TerrainPlateHideSunken 0 restores the stock behaviour (the plate always draws itself,
-//! and pokes through again at distance).
-int    g_terrainPlateHideSunken = 1;
-ICVar* g_pTerrainPlateHideSunkenCVar = nullptr;
-
-//! Metres of ground the plate has to be under before it is called buried. Deliberately smaller than the
-//! default Weld Sink (0.1 m), so that the outer ring of a welded plate - which is pulled up onto the
-//! terrain surface minus that sink on purpose - still passes.
-const float kSunkenHideClearance = 0.05f;
-//! ...and the clearance the test is repeated with once the plate IS hidden. The gap between the two is
-//! the hysteresis that stops a plate sitting exactly on the threshold from flickering between the two
-//! states on consecutive polls.
-const float kSunkenShowClearance = 0.f;
-//! Frames between two re-tests of a plate that is currently hidden. It is what notices the ground being
-//! sculpted away above a buried plate, and what repairs the flag if the slot render node was recreated
-//! by something other than our own rebuild.
-const uint32 kSunkenPollFrames = 16;
-//! Cap on the sample grid, i.e. on the cost of one decision. 257 x 257 at two samples per terrain unit
-//! covers a footprint of 128 terrain units; past that the test would have to sample coarser than the
-//! terrain itself and could no longer promise it had seen every bump, so it refuses to hide instead.
